    "id": 13,
    "text": "HDI",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### HUMAN DEVELOPMENT INDEX (HDI)\n\n#### 1. DEFINITION AND CONTEXT\nThe Human Development Index (HDI) is a composite statistical index developed by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) in 1990. Created by Pakistani economist Mahbub ul Haq and Indian Nobel laureate Amartya Sen, the HDI was designed to shift the focus of development economics from national income accounting to people-centered policies. It measures a country's average achievements in three basic dimensions of human development: health, knowledge, and standard of living.\n\n#### 2. THE THREE DIMENSIONS AND KEY INDICATORS\nThe HDI is constructed from three dimensions, each measured by specific indicators:\n1. **A Long and Healthy Life (Health)**:\n   * **Indicator**: **Life Expectancy at Birth**.\n   * **Interpretation**: The average number of years a newborn is expected to live if prevailing mortality patterns at the time of birth stay the same throughout its life.\n   * **Goalposts**: Minimum value is set at 20 years and maximum at 85 years.\n2. **Knowledge and Education (Education)**:\n   * Measured by a combination of two indicators:\n     * **Mean Years of Schooling**: The average number of years of education received by people aged 25 years and older during their lifetime (based on education attainment levels).\n     * **Expected Years of Schooling**: The number of years of schooling that a child of school-entrance age can expect to receive if prevailing patterns of age-specific enrollment rates persist throughout their life.\n   * **Goalposts**: Mean years of schooling (minimum 0, maximum 15); Expected years of schooling (minimum 0, maximum 18).\n3. **Decent Standard of Living (Income)**:\n   * **Indicator**: **Gross National Income (GNI) per capita** adjusted for **Purchasing Power Parity (PPP in US dollars)**.\n   * **Rationale**: GNI reflects the primary incomes of all residents plus net primary income from abroad. A logarithmic transformation of income is used because income has diminishing returns in terms of human capability.\n   * **Goalposts**: Minimum GNI per capita is $100 and maximum is $75,000.\n\n#### 3. STEP-BY-STEP CALCULATION OF HDI\nThe calculation of the HDI involves two main steps:\n* **Step 1: Calculating Dimension Indices**:\n  For each dimension, an index between 0 and 1 is calculated using the formula:\n  $$\\text{Dimension Index} = \\frac{\\text{Actual Value} - \\text{Minimum Value}}{\\text{Maximum Value} - \\text{Minimum Value}}$$\n  *For the Income Index, natural logarithms of the values are used prior to calculation.*\n* **Step 2: Calculating the Composite Index (HDI)**:\n  The HDI is the **Geometric Mean** of the three dimension indices:\n  $$\\text{HDI} = \\sqrt[3]{\\text{Health Index} \\times \\text{Education Index} \\times \\text{Income Index}}$$\n  *The geometric mean was adopted in 2010 to replace the arithmetic mean, as it penalizes poor performance in any single dimension and avoids assuming perfect substitutability between health, education, and income.*\n\n#### 4. CLASSIFICATION OF COUNTRIES BY HDI\nCountries are categorized into four levels of human development based on their HDI score:\n* **Very High Human Development**: HDI score of 0.800 or above.\n* **High Human Development**: HDI score between 0.700 and 0.799.\n* **Medium Human Development**: HDI score between 0.550 and 0.699.\n* **Low Human Development**: HDI score below 0.550.\n\n#### 5. INDIAN CONTEXT AND PROGRESS\nIndia is classified under the **Medium Human Development** category. In recent UNDP reports, India's HDI score is around 0.644, ranking it approximately 134th globally. Since 1990, India has shown significant progress, with life expectancy increasing by over 7 years and expected years of schooling rising. Key national programs contributing to this include the **National Health Mission (NHM)**, **Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan**, and **Poshan Abhiyaan**.\n\n#### 6. LIMITATIONS OF HDI\n* It is a composite average and hides inequalities within a country (remedied by the *Inequality-adjusted HDI* or IHDI).\n* It does not measure gender disparities (remedied by the *Gender Inequality Index* or GII).\n* It excludes ecological and environmental sustainability factors, although green/ecological adjustments are proposed.\n* It ignores political freedom, civil liberties, and human rights.",
