    "id": 145,
    "text": "Differences between marasmus and kwashiorker.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Differences between Kwashiorkor and Marasmus\n\nKwashiorkor and Marasmus are the two extreme clinical forms of Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM), commonly seen in children in developing nations.\n\n| Feature | Kwashiorkor | Marasmus |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Deficiency** | Severe **protein deficiency** with relatively adequate calorie intake (\"wet\" malnutrition). | Severe **calorie/energy deficiency** with protein deficiency (\"dry\" malnutrition). |\n| **Typical Age of Onset** | **1 to 3 years** (the post-weaning period, often triggered by the birth of a sibling). | **Under 1 year** (infancy, often due to early weaning or dilute bottle feeding). |\n| **Edema** | **Present** (bilateral, pitting edema starting in the feet and lower limbs, caused by hypoalbuminemia). | **Absent**. |\n| **Body Wasting** | Marked muscle wasting, but masked by edema and some subcutaneous fat. | Extreme wasting (\"skin and bones\"), with virtually no subcutaneous fat. |\n| **Facial Appearance** | **Moon face** (round, swollen face due to fluid retention). | **Old man or monkey facies** (wizened look due to the loss of buccal fat pads). |\n| **Mental State** | Markedly **apathetic**, lethargic, miserable, and difficult to comfort. | Alert, but highly **irritable** and hungry. |\n| **Hair Changes** | Very common: hair is sparse, thin, easily pluckable, and shows the **\"flag sign\"** (alternating bands of light and dark color). | Rare: hair may be dry and thin, but significant color changes are uncommon. |\n| **Skin Pathology** | Common: **\"flaky paint\"** or **\"crazy paving\"** dermatosis (hyperpigmentation and peeling of skin in pressure areas). | Rare: skin is dry, thin, and loose, showing redundant skin folds (especially in the gluteal region). |\n| **Hepatomegaly** | Common (fatty infiltration/steatosis of the liver due to reduced apolipoprotein synthesis). | Absent (no fat accumulation in the liver). |\n| **Serum Albumin** | Markedly reduced ($< 2.0\\text{ g/dL}$). | Normal or only slightly reduced. |",
    "page": 15
