    "id": 177,
    "text": "A)Enlist Vector Born Diseases. Describe MALARIA under the Following Headings: B) Epidemiology C) Life Cycle D)Malarial Indices (Annual Parasitic Incidence) E)Prevention And Control (Prophylaxis And Integrated Vector Management ) F)Malarial Vaccines                                               {Study In Conjugation With NVBDCP}",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 14,
    "answer": "### Malaria: Epidemiology, Life Cycle, Indices, and Integrated Management\n\nMalaria is a major vector-borne protozoan disease transmitted by the bite of infected female *Anopheles* mosquitoes. In India, it is a significant public health challenge managed under the **National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP)**, with the national goal of achieving a malaria-free India by 2027 and elimination by 2030.\n\n#### 1. Epidemiology\n*   **Agent Factors:** Caused by protozoa of the genus *Plasmodium*. In India, **_Plasmodium vivax_** (associated with relapses due to dormant liver stages) and **_Plasmodium falciparum_** (associated with severe, fatal complications like cerebral malaria) are the predominant species. *P. malariae* is rare, and *P. ovale* is extremely rare.\n*   **Vector Factors:** Transmitted by female **_Anopheles_** mosquitoes (e.g., *An. culicifacies* in rural areas, *An. stephensi* in urban areas). They breed in clean, stagnant water (rainwater pools, overhead tanks, coolers) and bite primarily between dusk and dawn.\n*   **Host Factors:** All ages are susceptible. Pregnant women, infants, and non-immune travelers are at high risk for severe disease. Splenectomized individuals are highly vulnerable.\n*   **Environmental Factors:** Transmission peaks during and immediately after the monsoon season (July to October), corresponding to vector breeding peaks.\n\n#### 2. Life Cycle of Plasmodium\n*   **Asexual Cycle (in Humans - Intermediate Host):**\n    1.  *Pre-Erythrocytic Schizogony:* Sporozoites injected by the mosquito bite migrate to the liver, invade hepatocytes, multiply, and release merozoites.\n    2.  *Exo-Erythrocytic Stage:* In *P. vivax*, some sporozoites remain dormant in the liver as **hypnozoites**, reactivating months later to cause clinical relapses.\n    3.  *Erythrocytic Schizogony:* Merozoites invade red blood cells, multiply, and cause RBC rupture, releasing pyrogens that trigger the classic clinical paroxysm (cold, hot, and sweating stages). Some trophozoites differentiate into male and female gametocytes.\n*   **Sexual Cycle (in Mosquitoes - Definitive Host):** Gametocytes ingested during a blood meal undergo fertilization in the mosquito midgut (sporogony), eventually producing sporozoites that migrate to the salivary glands.\n\n#### 3. Epidemiological Indices\n*   **Annual Parasite Incidence (API):** The number of confirmed malaria cases per 1,000 population per year. Used to stratify risk areas:\n    $$\\text{API} = \\frac{\\text{Total confirmed positive slides in a year}}{\\text{Total population under surveillance}} \\times 1,000$$\n*   **Annual Blood Examination Rate (ABER):** Measures surveillance adequacy. Ideally, ABER should be **$\\ge 10\\%$**:\n    $$\\text{ABER} = \\frac{\\text{Total blood slides examined}}{\\text{Total population under surveillance}} \\times 100$$\n*   **Slide Positivity Rate (SPR):** Percentage of examined slides that are positive:\n    $$\\text{SPR} = \\frac{\\text{Total positive slides}}{\\text{Total slides examined}} \\times 100$$\n\n#### 4. Prevention and Integrated Vector Management (IVM)\n*   **Integrated Vector Management (IVM):** Combining methods for maximum impact:\n    *   *Source Reduction:* Weekly cleaning of domestic water containers (\"Dry Day\"), filling ditches, and draining stagnant water.\n    *   *Biological Control:* Introducing larvivorous fish like **_Gambusia affinis_** or *Poecilia reticulata* in wells and ponds.\n    *   *Chemical Control:* Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) with synthetic pyrethroids or DDT on indoor walls. Applying chemical larvicides (Temephos) to non-potable water.\n*   **Personal Protection:** Use of **Long-Lasting Insecticidal Nets (LLINs)** and topical repellents (DEET).\n*   **Case Management:** Daily therapy with **Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT)** combined with a single dose of Primaquine for *P. falciparum*. Chloroquine for 3 days followed by Primaquine for 14 days (to eradicate hypnozoites) for *P. vivax*.\n*   **Chemoprophylaxis:** Recommended for travelers and military personnel (Doxycycline daily or Mefloquine weekly).",
