    "id": 180,
    "text": "JAPANESE ENCEPHALITIS: A)Epidemiology B)Prevention And Control. C)JE Vaccine(3marks) { NVBDCP}",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 7,
    "answer": "### Japanese Encephalitis (JE)\n\nJapanese Encephalitis (JE) is a serious, mosquito-borne viral zoonotic disease of public health significance in India, particularly in states like Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Assam, and West Bengal. It is the leading cause of epidemic viral encephalitis in Asia, carrying high mortality and long-term neurological disability rates.\n\n#### 1. Epidemiology\n*   **Agent Factors:** Caused by the Japanese Encephalitis Virus (JEV), a single-stranded RNA virus belonging to the family Flaviviridae.\n*   **Vector Factors:** Primarily transmitted by the female **_Culex tritaeniorhynchus_** mosquito (and related *Culex vishnui* group). They are night biters and breed extensively in flooded rice paddies, shallow ditches, and water hyacinth ponds.\n*   **Reservoir / Host System:**\n    *   *Pigs:* Act as the **amplifying host**. The virus multiplies rapidly in pigs to high titers without causing disease. Pig farms near human habitations are major risk factors.\n    *   *Ardeid Birds (Herons, Egrets):* Act as natural reservoir hosts, maintaining the bird-mosquito-bird transmission cycle.\n    *   *Humans and Horses:* Act as **dead-end hosts**. JEV viremia in humans is transient and of low titer, insufficient to reinfect biting mosquitoes.\n*   **Host Factors:** Primarily affects children under 15 years in endemic areas. Adults are usually immune due to subclinical exposure.\n*   **Seasonality:** Peak incidence occurs during and after the monsoon season, coinciding with high mosquito density.\n\n#### 2. Clinical Features and Diagnosis\n*   **Clinical Presentation:** The vast majority of infections are subclinical ($300:1$ ratio). Clinical cases present with sudden onset of high fever, severe headache, neck stiffness, altered sensorium, convulsions (highly common in children), tremors, spastic paralysis, and coma.\n*   **Prognosis:** The Case Fatality Rate (CFR) is approximately **25% to 30%** in clinical cases. Of the survivors, **30% to 50%** suffer permanent neurological or psychiatric sequelae (paralysis, epilepsy, speech disorders, cognitive impairment).\n*   **Diagnosis:** Detection of **JEV-specific IgM antibodies** in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) or serum using IgM Antibody Capture ELISA (MAC-ELISA).\n\n#### 3. Prevention and Control\n*   **Vector Control:** Insecticide spraying (IRS) in pig styes and houses, larviciding rice fields, and personal protection using LLINs.\n*   **Pig Management:** Vaccination of pigs in endemic areas and keeping pig styes away (minimum 5 km) from human settlements.\n*   **Human Immunization (JE Vaccines under UIP):**\n    *   *Live Attenuated Vaccine (SA 14-14-2):* The primary vaccine used in India's public health system in endemic districts. Administered subcutaneously as a 2-dose series: **1st dose at 9-12 months** (with MR-1) and **2nd dose at 16-24 months** (with MR-2). Dose is 0.5 mL.\n    *   *Inactivated Vaccine (JENVAC):* An indigenously developed Vero cell-derived vaccine, given in 2 doses; provides high immunogenicity.",
