    "text": "KALA AZAR (Leshmaniasis)Under The Following Headings: A) Epidemiology B)Types & Clinical Features C) Prevention And Control.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Kala-Azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis)\n\nKala-Azar (Visceral Leishmaniasis), also known as \"Dum Dum fever\" or \"black sickness,\" is a slow-progressing, severe systemic disease caused by protozoan parasites. In India, it is highly endemic in the Gangetic plains, particularly in Bihar, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh, and West Bengal. The national program targets elimination, defined as reducing the annual incidence to **$< 1$ case per 10,000 population** at the block level.\n\n#### 1. Epidemiology\n*   **Agent Factors:** Caused by the protozoan parasite **_Leishmania donovani_**. In the host's body, it exists as an intracellular, non-flagellated **amastigote (LD body)** within reticuloendothelial cells (spleen, liver, bone marrow).\n*   **Vector Factors:** Transmitted by the bite of the female **Sandfly (_Phlebotomus argentipes_)**. They are small, silent, weak fliers that hop rather than fly. They breed in damp, organic-rich soil, cracks in mud walls of houses, and cattle sheds.\n*   **Reservoir:** Humans are the only known reservoir in India (anthroponotic transmission). \n*   **Incubation Period:** Typically 2 to 6 months (range: 10 days to several years).\n\n#### 2. Clinical Features\n*   **Visceral Leishmaniasis:** Characterized by prolonged, irregular fever (often showing a double daily spike), significant weight loss, severe muscle wasting, hepatomegaly, and **massive, painless splenomegaly** (splenomegaly is progressive and can reach the right iliac fossa). Hyperpigmentation of the skin (especially on the face, hands, and abdomen, hence the name \"Kala-Azar\" or black fever) is common. Chronic bone marrow infiltration leads to pancytopenia (anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia) and secondary infections.\n*   **Post-Kala-Azar Dermal Leishmaniasis (PKDL):** A cutaneous manifestation that develops in 5% to 10% of cured Kala-Azar patients months or years after treatment. It presents as hypopigmented macules, erythematous patches, or nodular lesions containing parasites. PKDL patients are **highly infectious reservoirs** for sandflies and must be treated.\n\n#### 3. Diagnosis and Treatment\n*   **Diagnostic Methods:**\n    *   *rK39 Rapid Diagnostic Test:* An immunochromatographic dipstick test detecting antibodies against the recombinant K39 antigen. It is highly sensitive, specific, and used for rapid diagnosis in field settings.\n    *   *Definitive Diagnosis:* Demonstrating amastigotes (LD bodies) in splenic aspirates (very high yield but risk of hemorrhage) or bone marrow aspirates.\n*   **Treatment:**\n    *   *First-line:* **Liposomal Amphotericin B** (single IV dose of 10 mg/kg; highly effective, safe, and cures in 90%+ cases).\n    *   *Alternative:* **Miltefosine** (oral drug; taken for 28 days).\n*   **Vector Control:** Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) with synthetic pyrethroids (e.g., Deltamethrin) up to 6 feet on indoor mud walls to kill resting sandflies.",
