    "text": "Describe RABIES (hydrophobia): A) Epidemiology B) Add A Note On Wound Treatment. C) Pre Exposure And Post Exposure Prophylaxis . D)Vaccination For Rabies",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Rabies (Hydrophobia)\n\nRabies is an acute, highly fatal, progressive viral encephalitis caused by the Rabies virus. It is transmitted to humans by the bite, scratch, or lick of an infected rabid animal, primarily domestic dogs in India. Rabies has a **100% case fatality rate** once clinical symptoms develop, making prompt Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) a critical life-saving intervention.\n\n#### 1. Epidemiology\n*   **Agent Factors:** Rabies lyssavirus is a bullet-shaped, single-stranded RNA virus belonging to the Rhabdoviridae family. It is highly neurotropic, migrating from the site of entry along peripheral nerves to the brain at a rate of 12-24 mm/day.\n*   **Reservoir:** Domestic dogs are responsible for 97% of human rabies cases in India (urban rabies). Wild carnivores (jackals, foxes) and bats can also transmit the virus (sylvatic rabies).\n*   **Transmission:** Direct inoculation of virus-containing saliva into wounds via animal bites, scratches, or licks on broken skin or mucous membranes. Ocular transmission is rare.\n*   **Incubation Period:** Typically 1 to 3 months (range: 10 days to over a year). It is shorter if the bite site is highly innervated and close to the brain (e.g., face, neck, hands).\n*   **Clinical Stages:** Presents as prodromal symptoms (fever, pain/paresthesia at the bite site), progressing to **furious rabies** (characterized by agitation, hallucinations, aerophobia, and the classic **hydrophobia** â€“ painful spasms of laryngeal and pharyngeal muscles triggered by the sight or sound of water) or **dumb/paralytic rabies** (progressive flaccid paralysis). Death occurs within 2 to 7 days due to cardiorespiratory arrest.\n\n#### 2. Wound Treatment (Immediate and Critical)\nImmediate local wound management can prevent virus entry into the nervous system:\n1.  **Thorough Washing:** Flush the bite wound immediately with soap and running water for **at least 15 minutes**. Soap dissolves the lipid envelope of the virus, neutralizing it.\n2.  **Chemical Disinfection:** Apply virucidal agents like **70% Ethanol** or **Povidone-Iodine**.\n3.  **No Suturing:** Wounds must not be sutured. If suturing is clinically necessary (e.g., severe facial lacerations), it must be done loosely and only *after* infiltrating the wound with Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG).\n\n#### 3. Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) Protocol (WHO Guidelines)\nExposure is categorized into three types, each requiring specific treatment:\n\n*   **Category I:** Touching, feeding, or licks on intact skin. No treatment is needed.\n*   **Category II:** Minor scratches or abrasions without bleeding, or licks on broken skin.\n    *   *Treatment:* Immediate wound washing + **Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV)**.\n*   **Category III:** Single or multiple transdermal bites or scratches, licks on mucous membranes, or exposures to bats.\n    *   *Treatment:* Immediate wound washing + **Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV)** + **Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG)**.\n\n##### A. Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) Regimens:\nCell culture vaccines (e.g., Purified Vero Cell Rabies Vaccine - PVRV) are administered:\n*   *Intramuscular (IM) Essen Regimen:* 0.5 mL or 1.0 mL (dose depends on brand) injected in the deltoid muscle (never gluteal) on **days 0, 3, 7, 14, and 28**.\n*   *Intradermal (ID) Regimen (Thai Red Cross):* 2-site ID injection of 0.1 mL on **days 0, 3, 7, and 28** (highly cost-effective and preferred in India's public health system).\n\n##### B. Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG):\nProvides immediate passive neutralizing antibodies at the bite site. Administered on Day 0. The entire calculated dose must be infiltrated in and around the wound; any remaining portion is given IM. Doses:\n*   *Human RIG (HRIG):* $20\\text{ IU/kg}$ body weight.\n*   *Equine RIG (ERIG):* $40\\text{ IU/kg}$ body weight (requires skin sensitivity testing).",
