    "id": 185,
    "text": "A)Enlist Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) B)Describes Syndromic Management Of Reproductive Tract Infections Or Sexually Transmitted Infections (RTI/STI ) + HIV / AIDS  A.Epidemiology B. Problem Statement C.Who Clinical Staging Of HIV . D.HIV-TB Co-Infection. E. Chemoprophylaxis. F.Add A Note On NACO Program.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 12,
    "answer": "### Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) and Syndromic Management\n\nSexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) and Reproductive Tract Infections (RTIs) represent a major public health concern. They cause significant morbidity, including pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), ectopic pregnancy, infertility, cervical cancer, and increase the risk of sexual transmission of HIV by 3 to 10 times.\n\n---\n\n### A) ENLISTING SEXUALLY TRANSMITTED INFECTIONS (STIs)\nSTIs are classified based on the causative pathogen:\n1.  **Bacterial STIs:**\n    *   *Syphilis:* Caused by *Treponema pallidum*.\n    *   *Gonorrhea:* Caused by *Neisseria gonorrhoeae*.\n    *   *Chancroid:* Caused by *Haemophilus ducreyi*.\n    *   *Chlamydiasis:* Caused by *Chlamydia trachomatis*.\n2.  **Viral STIs:**\n    *   *HIV/AIDS:* Caused by Human Immunodeficiency Virus.\n    *   *Genital Herpes:* Caused by Herpes Simplex Virus Type 2 (HSV-2).\n    *   *Genital Warts:* Caused by Human Papillomavirus (HPV).\n    *   *Hepatitis B:* Caused by Hepatitis B Virus (HBV).\n3.  **Parasitic and Fungal STIs:**\n    *   *Trichimoniasis:* Caused by *Trichomonas vaginalis* (protozoan).\n    *   *Candidiasis:* Caused by *Candida albicans* (fungal).\n    *   *Scabies & Pediculosis pubis:* Ectoparasitic infestations.\n\n---\n\n### B) SYNDROMIC MANAGEMENT OF RTIs/STIs\nSyndromic management is a clinical approach where diagnoses are based on identifying a group of consistent symptoms and signs (syndromes) rather than laboratory tests. The patient is treated immediately for all major pathogens capable of causing that syndrome.\n\n#### NACO Color-Coded STI/RTI Kits:\nNational AIDS Control Organisation (NACO) in India provides seven color-coded kits containing pre-packaged, standardized drug regimens for specific syndromes:\n\n*   **KIT 1 - GREY (Urethral, Cervical, or Anorectal Discharge):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* *N. gonorrhoeae* and *C. trachomatis*.\n    *   *Drugs:* **Tab Cefixime (400 mg)** single oral dose + **Tab Azithromycin (1 g)** single oral dose.\n*   **KIT 2 - GREEN (Vaginal Discharge):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* *T. vaginalis*, *Candida albicans*, and Bacterial Vaginosis.\n    *   *Drugs:* **Tab Secnidazole (2 g)** single oral dose + **Cap Fluconazole (150 mg)** single oral dose.\n*   **KIT 3 - WHITE (Genital Ulcer Disease - Non-Herpetic):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* *T. pallidum* (Syphilis) and *H. ducreyi* (Chancroid).\n    *   *Drugs:* **Inj Benzathine Penicillin G (2.4 million units)** IM single dose + **Tab Azithromycin (1 g)** single oral dose.\n*   **KIT 4 - BLUE (Genital Ulcer Disease - Penicillin Allergic):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* Syphilis and Chancroid in patients allergic to penicillin.\n    *   *Drugs:* **Cap Doxycycline (100 mg)** twice daily for 15 days + **Tab Azithromycin (1 g)** single oral dose.\n*   **KIT 5 - RED (Genital Ulcer Disease - Herpetic):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* Herpes Simplex Virus Type 2.\n    *   *Drugs:* **Tab Acyclovir (400 mg)** three times daily for 7 days.\n*   **KIT 6 - YELLOW (Lower Abdominal Pain / Pelvic Inflammatory Disease):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* Mixed polymicrobial aerobic/anaerobic endometritis/salpingitis.\n    *   *Drugs:* **Tab Cefixime (400 mg)** single dose + **Tab Metronidazole (400 mg)** twice daily for 14 days + **Cap Doxycycline (100 mg)** twice daily for 14 days.\n*   **KIT 7 - BLACK (Scrotal Swelling):**\n    *   *Pathogens covered:* *N. gonorrhoeae* and *C. trachomatis* (epididymo-orchitis).\n    *   *Drugs:* **Tab Cefixime (400 mg)** single dose + **Cap Doxycycline (100 mg)** twice daily for 14 days.\n\n#### Advantages of Syndromic Management:\n1.  **Immediate Treatment:** Administered during the first visit, preventing patient drop-out while waiting for lab tests.\n2.  **Highly Cost-Effective:** No expensive diagnostic tests or specialized laboratories required.\n3.  **Feasibility:** Easily implemented by primary care doctors, ANMs, and nurses at rural clinics.\n4.  **Partner Notification:** Promotes treating sexual partners simultaneously to break the transmission loop.",
