    "id": 189,
    "text": "Epidemiology, Clinical Features Prevention & Control, Complications OF MEASLES.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Measles (Rubeola): Epidemiology, Clinical Stages, Complications, and Prevention\n\nMeasles is an acute, highly infectious viral disease characterized by high fever, respiratory symptoms, Koplik's spots, and a generalized maculopapular rash. It is one of the leading causes of vaccine-preventable death in young children globally. The national program in India targets the elimination of Measles and Rubella (MR) through mass immunization.\n\n#### 1. Epidemiology\n*   **Agent Factors:** Caused by the Measles virus, an RNA virus belonging to the genus Morbillivirus in the Paramyxoviridae family. Humans are the only reservoir. The virus is highly unstable in the environment, easily destroyed by heat, light, and disinfectants.\n*   **Host Factors:** Primarily affects children between **6 months and 3 years** of age in unvaccinated populations. Infants are protected up to 6-9 months by transplacental maternal antibodies.\n*   **Transmission:** Highly infectious ($R_0$ is 12 to 18). Spread by **respiratory droplets** and airborne transmission (suspended droplet nuclei in enclosed spaces can remain infectious for up to 2 hours).\n*   **Incubation Period:** 10 to 14 days.\n*   **Communicability:** From **4 days before** to **4 days after** the appearance of the rash.\n\n#### 2. Clinical Stages\n*   **Prodromal Stage (3-4 days):** High fever, coryza (runny nose), dry barky cough, and conjunctivitis with photophobia (the **\"3 Cs\"**).\n    *   *Koplik's Spots:* Pathognomonic lesions appearing 1-2 days before the rash. They are tiny, bluish-white spots on an erythematous base on the buccal mucosa, opposite the lower second molars.\n*   **Eruptive Stage (4-6 days):** Maculopapular rash starting behind the ears and along the hairline, spreading downward to the face, neck, trunk, and extremities over 3 days (centrifugal spread). The fever peaks when the rash reaches the feet. The rash fades in the same order of appearance, leaving brownish desquamation.\n*   **Post-Measles Stage:** Characterized by prolonged weight loss, weakness, and temporary immunosuppression (measles anergy) lasting several weeks, making children highly susceptible to secondary infections.\n\n#### 3. Complications\n*   *Respiratory:* Bronchopneumonia (most common cause of measles-associated death in malnourished children), croup, and otitis media.\n*   *Gastrointestinal:* Severe diarrhea (which can precipitate acute malnutrition), mouth ulcers (stomatitis).\n*   *Ocular:* Keratomalacia and blindness (measles rapidly depletes liver Vitamin A stores).\n*   *Neurological:* Acute encephalitis; and **Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis (SSPE)** â€“ a rare, fatal, slow-virus brain degeneration occurring 5 to 10 years after primary infection.\n\n#### 4. Prevention and Control\n*   **Immunization (MR/MMR Vaccine under UIP):**\n    *   *1st Dose:* Administered at **9 to 12 months** of age (as Measles-Rubella [MR] vaccine, subcutaneously, 0.5 mL in the right upper arm).\n    *   *2nd Dose:* Administered at **16 to 24 months** of age.\n*   **Vitamin A Supplementation (Critical Intervention):**\n    *   Administer oral Vitamin A to all children diagnosed with measles to prevent corneal damage and reduce mortality by 50%.\n    *   *Dose:* **100,000 IU** (1 mL) for infants $<12$ months; **200,000 IU** (2 mL) for children $\\ge 12$ months. Give the first dose immediately and the second dose the next day.\n*   **Isolation:** Isolate the child at home for 4 days after the rash appears.",
