    "id": 257,
    "text": "Integrated Child Development Services(ICDS)-beneficiaries and Benefits",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### INTEGRATED CHILD DEVELOPMENT SERVICES (ICDS)\n\n### A) INTRODUCTION\nLaunched on **2nd October 1975**, the Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS) scheme is one of the world's largest flagship programs for early childhood care and development. The focal point of the program is the **Anganwadi Centre (AWC)**, managed by an Anganwadi Worker (AWW) and an Anganwadi Helper, serving a population of approximately **400 to 800 people** (with lower norms for tribal areas).\n\n---\n\n### B) BENEFICIARIES AND BENEFITS (THE 6 CORE SERVICES)\nThe ICDS scheme provides an integrated package of six services designed to target maternal and child health, nutrition, and education:\n\n| Service | Target Beneficiaries | Service Provider |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Supplementary Nutrition** | â€¢ Children 6 months to 6 years<br>â€¢ Pregnant and Lactating mothers | Anganwadi Worker & Helper |\n| **2. Immunization** | â€¢ Children under 6 years<br>â€¢ Pregnant women | ANM / Health Worker (Health Dept) |\n| **3. Health Check-up** | â€¢ Children under 6 years<br>â€¢ Pregnant & Lactating mothers | ANM / Medical Officer / AWW |\n| **4. Referral Services** | â€¢ Sick/Malnourished children<br>â€¢ High-risk pregnant women | AWW / ANM / Medical Officer |\n| **5. Non-formal Pre-school Education** | â€¢ Children 3 to 6 years | Anganwadi Worker |\n| **6. Nutrition & Health Education** | â€¢ Women aged 15-45 years | AWW / ANM / Lady Health Visitor |\n\n---\n\n### C) SUPPLEMENTARY NUTRITION GUIDELINES AND NORMS\nThe Supplementary Nutrition Program (SNP) is a core component, designed to bridge the nutritional gap between the actual intake and the recommended dietary allowances (RDA). The food is provided either as a Hot Cooked Meal at the center or as Take-Home Rations (THR).\n\n#### Nutritional Feeding Norms per Day:\n\n| Beneficiary Category | Daily Caloric Target (kcal) | Daily Protein Target (g) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Children (6 months to 72 months)** | 500 | 12 - 15 |\n| **Severely Malnourished Children** | 800 | 20 - 25 |\n| **Pregnant and Lactating Mothers** | 600 | 18 - 20 |\n\n---\n\n### D) ROLE OF FRONT-LINE WORKERS IN ICDS\n\n#### 1. Anganwadi Worker (AWW)\nThe AWW is a voluntary community-level worker selected from the local village. Her responsibilities include:\n* Organizing supplementary feeding sessions daily for children and mothers.\n* Conducting non-formal pre-school activities for children aged 3-6 years.\n* **Growth Monitoring**: Weighing children monthly, plotting their weights on the growth chart, and identifying malnourished children.\n* Implementing health and nutrition education sessions for mothers.\n* Assisting the ANM in organizing immunization and health check-up days (VHND).\n* Maintaining registers of births, deaths, and eligible beneficiaries in the village.\n\n#### 2. Anganwadi Helper\n* Cooking and serving the supplementary meals.\n* Maintaining cleanliness of the Anganwadi Centre.\n* Bringing young children from their homes to the Anganwadi Centre.",
