    "id": 264,
    "text": "Write short note on School health programme",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### SCHOOL HEALTH PROGRAMME\n\n### A) INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVES\nThe School Health Programme is an essential branch of community medicine designed to promote, protect, and maintain the health of school-going children and school personnel.\n* **Target Group**: School children represent a highly accessible, receptive, and structured segment of the population (nearly 20-25% of the total population).\n* **Primary Objectives**:\n  1. Early detection and treatment of physical, mental, and developmental defects.\n  2. Prevention and control of communicable diseases in the school community.\n  3. Provision of a safe, sanitary, and healthy school environment.\n  4. Building positive health habits and health awareness among children.\n\n---\n\n### B) CORE COMPONENTS OF THE SCHOOL HEALTH PROGRAMME\n\n#### 1. Health Appraisal and Screenings\n* **Periodic Medical Examinations**: Conducted annually by a visiting medical team (doctors and ophthalmic assistants). In India, this is integrated under the **RBSK (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram)**.\n* **Screening for \\\"4 Ds\\\"**:\n  * *Defects at birth*: Congenital heart disease, cleft lip.\n  * *Deficiencies*: Anaemia, vitamin A deficiency, goiter, malnutrition.\n  * *Diseases*: Skin infections, dental caries, otitis media, tuberculosis.\n  * *Developmental delays*: Learning disabilities, vision and hearing impairment.\n* **Daily Morning Inspection**: Conducted by the class teacher to detect early signs of infectious diseases (skin rashes, red eyes, coughing) or poor personal hygiene.\n\n#### 2. Treatment and Referral Services\n* Setting up a school health clinic or first-aid room.\n* Providing simple treatments for common ailments (deworming tablets, iron supplements).\n* Referral of children with complex conditions to nearby PHCs/CHCs.\n\n#### 3. Nutritional Services (Mid-Day Meal Scheme)\n* Provision of a free, hot cooked meal daily to children in government schools.\n* **Nutritional Standards per Day (PM POSHAN guidelines)**:\n  * Primary (Class 1-5): **450 calories and 12g protein**.\n  * Upper Primary (Class 6-8): **700 calories and 20g protein**.\n  * Weekly supplementation of Iron-Folic Acid (WIFS) and biannual deworming (Albendazole).\n\n#### 4. Prevention and Control of Communicable Diseases\n* Ensuring all children are immunized (especially booster doses of Td - Tetanus-Diphtheria vaccine at 10 and 16 years).\n* Quarantining children with contagious diseases (chickenpox, measles, conjunctivitis).\n\n#### 5. Healthy School Environment\nIPHS guidelines for school design:\n* *Classrooms*: Adequate floor space ($10 \\text{ sq ft}$ per student), proper ventilation, and natural lighting.\n* *Sanitation*: Separate, clean toilets for boys and girls (ratio of 1 toilet per 50 students).\n* *Water*: Safe, continuous drinking water supply.\n* *Playground*: Adequate open space for physical activity.\n\n#### 6. Health Education\n* Teaching children personal hygiene (handwashing, brushing teeth), environmental sanitation, healthy dietary habits, and physical education.\n* Introducing age-appropriate sexuality and mental health education in higher classes.",
