    "id": 288,
    "text": "Accredited social health activist (ASHA)-criteria,Role and Responsibilities.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Care of the Community",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### ACCREDITED SOCIAL HEALTH ACTIVIST (ASHA)\n\n### A) INTRODUCTION\nThe Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) is a key community-level health worker deployed under the **National Health Mission (NHM)** to act as the interface between the rural community and the public health system.\n* **Coverage**: Typically, **1 ASHA per 1,000 population** (or per habitation in sparse, tribal, or hilly regions).\n\n---\n\n### B) SELECTION CRITERIA FOR ASHA\nAn ASHA is selected from the local community based on strict criteria to ensure trust and availability:\n* **Gender**: Must be a **female resident of the village**.\n* **Marital Status**: Preferably married, widowed, or divorced (to ensure she remains in the village and is socially accepted by local women).\n* **Age**: Aged between **25 and 45 years**.\n* **Education**: Literate, with a minimum formal education up to **Class 8** (preferably Class 10) to enable recording of vital statistics and communication.\n* **Selection Process**: Selected through a participatory process involving the Gram Sabha, Anganwadi Workers, local self-help groups, and approved by the Block Health Team.\n\n---\n\n### C) ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES of ASHA\nAn ASHA performs five primary functions:\n\n#### 1. Community Health Mobilizer & Educator\n* Creating awareness in the village regarding personal hygiene, sanitation, safe drinking water, healthy nutrition, and the availability of government health services.\n* Mobilizing community members (especially women and children) for immunization sessions and health checkups on **Village Health Sanitation and Nutrition Days (VHSND)**.\n* Promoting delayed age of marriage and birth spacing.\n\n#### 2. Maternal and Child Health Activist\n* Identifying pregnant women in the village and facilitating early antenatal registration at the Sub-center/PHC.\n* Accompanying and facilitating institutional deliveries, specifically helping women access cash benefits under the **Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)**.\n* Conducting **Home-Based Newborn Care (HBNC)** visits (6 visits for institutional births, 7 visits for home births) to assess neonatal weight, check temperature, support exclusive breastfeeding, and screen for danger signs.\n\n#### 3. Depot Holder for Essential Supplies\n* Maintaining a first-aid and medical kit at her home containing essential supplies for immediate distribution: ORS packets, Zinc tablets, iron-folic acid tablets, oral contraceptive pills (Mala-N, Chhaya), condoms, sanitary napkins, and basic medicines (paracetamol).\n\n#### 4. Treatment Provider and Surveillance Agent\n* Acting as a Directly Observed Treatment Short-course (DOTS) provider for tuberculosis patients in her village, ensuring drug compliance.\n* Assisting in active surveillance for leprosy and vector-borne diseases (preparing blood slides for malaria).\n\n#### 5. Vital Statistics Reporter\n* Documenting and reporting births, deaths, and unusual disease outbreaks in the village to the ANM and PHC. She is compensated via performance-linked incentives.",
