    "text": "NATIONAL AIDS CONTROL PROGRAMME.(NACP) -TARGETS - Strategies -Organization -Indicators b) Integrated Counseling and Testing Centres (ICTC) c)Care, support and treatment in HIV.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Programmes in India",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### National AIDS Control Programme (NACP)\n\nThe National AIDS Control Programme (NACP) was launched by the Government of India in **1992** to control the transmission of HIV/AIDS. Currently, the program is in its fifth phase, **NACP-V (2021-2026)**, which aims to consolidate the gains of the previous phases and accelerate the elimination of the HIV epidemic as a public health threat by 2030.\n\n---\n\n### Part A: Targets, Strategies, Organisation, and Indicators\n\n#### 1. Targets:\n* **The 95-95-95 Targets**:\n  * **95%** of all people living with HIV (PLHIV) should know their HIV status.\n  * **95%** of all diagnosed PLHIV should receive sustained antiretroviral therapy (ART).\n  * **95%** of all people receiving ART should achieve viral suppression (undetectable viral load).\n* **Reduction Target**: 80% reduction in annual new HIV infections relative to the 2010 baseline.\n* **Mortality Target**: 80% reduction in AIDS-related deaths.\n* **Zero Transmission Target**: Achieve and maintain **Elimination of Mother-to-Child Transmission (EMTCT)** of HIV and Syphilis.\n* **Zero Discrimination**: Complete elimination of stigma and discrimination against PLHIV.\n\n#### 2. Strategies:\n* **Prevention Strategies (Targeted Interventions - TIs)**:\n  * Focusing on **High-Risk Groups (HRGs)**: Female Sex Workers (FSWs), Men who have sex with Men (MSM), Injecting Drug Users (IDUs), and Transgender individuals.\n  * Activities include: Free condom distribution, needle-syringe exchange programs, **Opioid Substitution Therapy (OST)** for IDUs, and treatment of Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs).\n* **IEC and Behavior Change**:\n  * Educating youth (red ribbon clubs in schools/colleges) and fighting stigma.\n* **Diagnostic Expansion**:\n  * Universal screening of pregnant women, voluntary counseling, and testing.\n* **\"Treat All\" Policy**:\n  * Immediate initiation of life-long Antiretroviral Therapy (ART) for all individuals who test positive for HIV, regardless of their CD4 count or clinical stage. First-line therapy is the Dolutegravir-based daily single-pill regimen (**TLD**: Tenofovir + Lamivudine + Dolutegravir).\n\n#### 3. Organisation:\n* **National Level**: National AIDS Control Organisation (NACO), a division of the MoHFW, which serves as the national policy-making and coordinating body.\n* **State Level**: State AIDS Control Societies (SACS), responsible for implementing NACO guidelines at the state level.\n* **District Level**: District AIDS Prevention and Control Units (DAPCU), prioritizing districts with high HIV workloads.\n* **Peripheral Service Centers**: ICTCs, ART Centres, Link ART Centres, and Care and Support Centres.\n\n#### 4. Key Indicators:\n* **HIV Prevalence Rate**: The percentage of the population living with HIV.\n* **Annual New Infections**: The estimated number of new HIV infections per year.\n* **ART Coverage Rate**: The percentage of diagnosed PLHIV actively receiving ART.\n* **Viral Suppression Rate**: The percentage of patients on ART with viral loads < 1,000 copies/mL.\n\n---\n\n### Part B: Integrated Counseling and Testing Centres (ICTC)\n\nAn ICTC is a health facility where a person is counseled and tested for HIV of their own free will (voluntary) or as advised by a medical provider.\n\n#### 1. Types of ICTCs:\n* **Stand-alone ICTCs**: Located in tertiary hospitals, medical colleges, and district hospitals, staffed by dedicated counselors and laboratory technicians.\n* **Facility Integrated ICTCs (F-ICTCs)**: Set up in primary health centers (PHCs) and community health centers (CHCs) to integrate HIV testing with general healthcare.\n* **Mobile ICTCs**: Vans equipped with testing kits and counselors that travel to remote areas, weekly markets, or transit sites to reach high-risk populations.\n\n#### 2. Core Functions of ICTCs:\n* **Pre-Test Counseling**:\n  * Educating the client on HIV transmission, assessing individual risk behaviors, obtaining informed written consent, and preparing the client for the result.\n* **Confidential HIV Testing**:\n  * Done using rapid test kits. NACO guidelines mandate a **three-test algorithm** using different antigen preparations/principles. A diagnosis is only confirmed positive if three different tests yield positive results.\n* **Post-Test Counseling**:\n  * **Negative Result**: Explaining the window period, reinforcing prevention strategies, and distributing condoms.\n  * **Positive Result**: Providing immediate psychosocial support, explaining that HIV is manageable, and physically linking the patient to the nearest ART Centre.\n* **Prevention of Parent-to-Child Transmission (PPTCT)**:\n  * Universal screening of pregnant women during ANC visits. Positive mothers are started on life-long ART (TLD) and newborns are given prophylactic Nevirapine syrup for 6-12 weeks to prevent vertical transmission.\n\n---\n\n### Part C: Care, Support, and Treatment (CST) in HIV\n\nCare, Support, and Treatment (CST) services are designed to improve the longevity and quality of life of PLHIV through medical, psychological, and social interventions.\n\n#### 1. Antiretroviral Therapy (ART) Centres:\n* Established in district and tertiary hospitals.\n* Provide comprehensive medical evaluation, free CD4 count testing, viral load testing, and free distribution of ART drugs.\n* Employs medical officers, counselors, and peer coordinators to monitor clinical progression and treatment adherence.\n\n#### 2. Link ART Centres (LACs):\n* Set up in peripheral hospitals (CHCs/sub-district hospitals) to act as satellite extensions of main ART Centres.\n* They facilitate easy monthly medicine refills and tracking of patients close to their residences, reducing travel costs and default rates.\n\n#### 3. Prophylaxis and Management of Opportunistic Infections (OIs):\n* Free screening and treatment for tuberculosis (the leading cause of death among PLHIV in India).\n* Provision of **Cotrimoxazole Preventive Therapy (CPT)** to patients with CD4 counts < 350 cells/mmÃ‚Â³ to prevent *Pneumocystis jirovecii* pneumonia and toxoplasmosis.\n\n#### 4. Care and Support Centres (CSCs):\n* Run by networks of people living with HIV (e.g., the *Vihaan* project).\n* They provide peer support, counseling, nutritional advice, disclosure support, and actively track \"lost to follow-up\" patients to bring them back into treatment.\n* They help patients link with social welfare schemes (like travel concessions, pensions, and educational support for children).",
