    "text": "a)Vision 2020: Right to Sight b)Salient features of National Programme for Control of Blindness",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Programmes in India",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### VISION 2020 AND THE NATIONAL PROGRAMME FOR CONTROL OF BLINDNESS\n\n### A) VISION 2020: THE RIGHT TO SIGHT\nVISION 2020: The Right to Sight was a global joint initiative launched in 1999 by the World Health Organization (WHO) and the International Agency for the Prevention of Blindness (IAPB) to eliminate avoidable (preventable and curable) blindness globally by the year 2020.\n\n#### 1. Priority Disease Areas\nThe program targeted five primary causes of avoidable blindness:\n* **Cataract**: High-volume, high-quality cataract surgery (IOL implantation).\n* **Refractive Errors and Low Vision**: School eye screening and provision of low-cost corrective spectacles.\n* **Childhood Blindness**: Immunization (measles), Vitamin A supplementation, and early surgery for congenital cataracts.\n* **Trachoma**: Implementing the **SAFE Strategy** (Surgery, Antibiotics, Facial cleanliness, Environmental hygiene).\n* **Diabetic Retinopathy & Glaucoma**: Early screening and laser/medical management.\n\n#### 2. Key Strategies\n* **Human Resource Development**: Training multidisciplinary eye care teams (ophthalmologists, optometrists, ophthalmic assistants).\n* **Infrastructure Development**: Setting up primary Vision Centres, secondary eye care hospitals, and tertiary centers of excellence.\n* **Technology Access**: Making diagnostic equipment and consumables (IOLs, sutures) affordable.\n\n---\n\n### B) NATIONAL PROGRAMME FOR CONTROL OF BLINDNESS & VISUAL IMPAIRMENT (NPCBVI)\nLaunched in India in **1976** as a 100% centrally sponsored scheme, it has been integrated under the National Health Mission.\n\n#### 1. Objectives and Targets\n* To reduce the prevalence of blindness in India from $1.4\\\\%$ (historically) to **below 0.3%**.\n* To provide comprehensive, high-quality eye care services at all levels of healthcare.\n\n#### 2. Salient Features and Activities\n* **Free Cataract Surgery**: High-volume cataract surgery with Intraocular Lens (IOL) implantation, transition from SICS (Small Incision Cataract Surgery) to Phacoemulsification, primarily in public hospitals and partnered NGO clinics.\n* **School Eye Screening (SES)**: Mobile ophthalmic teams screen school children for refractive errors and distribute **free corrective spectacles** to poor children.\n* **Control of Corneal Blindness**:\n  * Strengthening Eye Banks and Keratoplasty (corneal transplant) centers.\n  * Promoting eye donation campaigns (National Eye Donation Fortnight: 25th August to 8th September).\n* **Vision Centres at PHCs**: Establishing primary eye screening rooms at PHCs equipped with Ophthalmic Assistants and tele-ophthalmology links.\n* **Diabetic Retinopathy Screening**: Non-mydriatic fundus camera screenings at District NCD clinics.\n* **Financial Assistance to NGOs**: Reimbursing NGO hospitals for conducting free eye care camps and surgeries in underserved areas.",
